 "checked_text": "we start at a, and breadth-first search uses a fifo queue: first in, first out. i'll circle a and write the initial queue, because a is marked seen before any visiting happens.\nqueue [a]\n\nnow dequeue a and visit it first. its alphabetical neighbours are b, c, e, so they enter the queue in that order as level 1 children of a.\nvisit 1 ✓\nseen, l1\nseen, l1\nqueue [b,c,e]\n\nfifo means b comes out next, not c or e yet. from b, the unvisited alphabetical neighbours are d then f, so both are marked and placed at the back.\nvisit 2 ✓\nseen, l2\nseen, l2\nqueue [c,e,d,f]\n\nnext the queue front is c, so c is visited third. c's only unvisited neighbour is g, so g joins after d and f, not before them.\nvisit 3 ✓\nseen, l2\nqueue [e,d,f,g]\n\nnow bfs simply drains the queue: e, then d, then f, then g. none adds a new unvisited node, so the bfs order is a, b, c, e, d, f, g.\nvisit 4 ✓\nvisit 5 ✓\nvisit 6 ✓\nvisit 7 ✓\n\ndepth-first search would use the same alphabetical choice, but it dives before siblings: from a to b to d, then backtrack to f, then c to g, then e. so dfs order is a, b, d, f, c, g, e.\ndfs dives\nbfs: a b c e d\ndfs: a b d f c\nstart a -> bfs: queue\nbfs: queue -> visit front\nvisit front -> enqueue neighbours\nenqueue neighbours -> queue empty?\nqueue empty? -> (no) visit front\nqueue empty? -> (yes) done"
